-#}
Entry condition: the human merged the release MR (`prep-release` → `main`)
on GitLab — or a relaunch derived a leg-2 step from run state. Everything in
this leg keys on ONE fact: the release-MR merge SHA recorded in run state.
No step acts on an earlier leg's memory, the working tree, or a branch tip
that could move — a step either verifies its target against the recorded
merge SHA or it does not run.

On entry (and on every re-entry) run `work release status` first: it prints
the recorded fields, the derived leg, and the single next step. Record each
step's phase before starting it (`work state set --phase=<step name>`, using
the step names below). Two of these names — `leg2-record-merge` and
`leg2-preconditions` — extend the resume decision table's derived-step
vocabulary without ever appearing as a derived next step: `work release
status` folds them into its rows. A derivation of `leg2-create-tag`
therefore runs steps 1 through 3: step 1 as an idempotent re-record (its
mismatch check runs every time, even when the gate already recorded the
merge SHA), then step 2's preconditions, then the tag.

Credentials are provisioned, never fetched: the GitHub fine-grained PAT
arrives through this session's credential env as `LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN`,
and the release SSH signing key is mounted read-only at
`/release-signing-key` (the path is also exported as
`LMER_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY`); `release.signing_key` is the REFERENCE resolved
in Phase 0.5. Consume these deliveries as-is — do NOT hunt for keys
elsewhere, generate one, or re-implement provisioning. A missing or rejected
credential is a hard error naming the credential requirement (see HARD
RULES), never a workaround.

`release.github_target` is a bare `https://` URL and the container carries no
persistent credential helper for the mirror host, with `~/.gitconfig` a
read-only mount. Every operation against the `github` remote — the fetch in
step 2, both `ls-remote` checks, and both `bin/gate-push` invocations —
therefore fails unauthenticated. Supply the PAT through lmer's action-free
ASKPASS helper. The helper only answers Git's credential prompts from its own
environment; it performs no network or filesystem action.

Supply it PER COMMAND, never once for the leg: each command runs in a fresh
shell, so an `export` in one invocation does not reach the next. The prefix
below goes in front of EACH `github`-remote command, in the same shell
invocation as that command — the step-2 fetch, both `ls-remote` checks, and
both `bin/gate-push` invocations (`bin/gate-push` shells out to `git`, so
ASKPASS reaches Git through the environment it inherits):

```
# `x-access-token` is the fixed username the forge expects for a PAT; the
# provisioned token is the password half. LC_ALL=C makes Git's ASKPASS prompt
# vocabulary match the helper's audited Username/Password contract.
(
  export LC_ALL=C
  export GIT_ASKPASS=/Agents/global/bin/lmer-git-askpass
  export GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0
  export LMER_GIT_ASKPASS_USERNAME=x-access-token
  export LMER_GIT_ASKPASS_PASSWORD="${LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN}"
  <the github-remote command>
)
```

The subshell keeps ASKPASS scoped to the one command that needs it, and
`export` is a builtin, so the token never reaches any process's command line.
Never write the token into the remote URL, a config file, or run state. In
particular, do not use numbered Git config around `bin/gate-push`: the gate
runs pytest in its inherited environment, and tests own their isolated Git
config entries.

### Step 1 — `leg2-record-merge` — record the merge SHA

The anchor for every later step. Skipping it or reordering it breaks the
whole ladder.

- [ ] Read the release MR's merge SHA from GitLab (`gitlab-review` on the
      release MR — the merge commit on `main`). Full 40-hex, never
      abbreviated.
- [ ] Re-read the version AT THAT SHA — never the working tree, never leg
      1's memory: `git fetch origin` then
      `git show <merge-sha>:pyproject.toml`, extracting `project.version`
      from that exact blob.
- [ ] Record both together:
      `work release record merge-sha <merge-sha> --version <observed-version>`

The recorder HARD STOPS when the observed version disagrees with leg 1's
recorded version. That mismatch means a second bump or a foreign commit
landed on `main` — a human decision is required, not a repair attempt.
Record it before ending the session:
`work state set --stop-reason=critical_error --critical-error
'{"summary": "version mismatch at merge SHA", "detail": "leg 1 recorded <recorded> but pyproject.toml at <merge-sha> says <observed>"}'`
then `work commit` and end the session. Re-recording the same SHA and
version on a resume is safe — the mismatch check runs even on an idempotent
re-record.

### Step 2 — `leg2-preconditions` — preconditions, each a receipt

Both checks run through `work verify` so a receipt exists, not just prose:

- [ ] GitLab `main` pipeline at the merge SHA is green. Resolve the
      pipeline FOR the merge SHA first (GitLab API
      `/projects/<id>/pipelines?sha=<merge-sha>` — always pinned to the
      merge SHA, never "latest on main"), then record the receipt:
      `work verify gitlab-main-pipeline -- gitlab-pipeline <project> <pipeline-id> --host <hostname> --expect-status success`
      (`--expect-status success` makes the exit code carry the verdict — a
      red or still-running pipeline exits non-zero, so the receipt can
      never record a green checkmark for a red pipeline.)
      Red or still running → do not proceed; wait or investigate.
- [ ] GitHub `main` is an ancestor of the merge SHA. Add the mirror remote
      once (`git remote add github <release.github_target>` — skip if
      already present), fetch it with the credential prefix above in the
      same shell invocation as the fetch, then:
      `work verify github-ancestry -- git merge-base --is-ancestor <github-main-sha> <merge-sha>`
      where `<github-main-sha>` is `git rev-parse github/main` after the
      fetch.

GitHub `main` holding any commit that is NOT an ancestor of the merge SHA is
divergence — a HARD STOP with a documented remediation path, NEVER a
force-push. Record a critical error naming both SHAs and the fork point
(`git merge-base github/main <merge-sha>`), `work commit`, end the session.
The remediation path is a human one: inspect what landed on the mirror
out-of-band, remove or merge it deliberately on the canonical GitLab side,
and relaunch the release — leg 2 re-derives from run state and re-checks.

### Step 3 — `leg2-create-tag` — the SSH-signed tag, at exactly the merge SHA

The tag name is `release.tag_prefix` + the version re-read in step 1 at the
merge SHA — and Phase 0.5 pins `tag_prefix` to exactly `"v"`, so the name is
always `v<version>` (the kernel's `work release record tag` accepts nothing
else) — never a name remembered from leg 1.

- [ ] Idempotency check FIRST — does the tag already exist? Check locally
      and on both remotes (`git ls-remote origin refs/tags/<tag>`,
      `git ls-remote github refs/tags/<tag>` — the `github` one carrying the
      credential prefix in its own shell invocation). If it exists:
      - pointing at exactly the recorded merge SHA AND carrying a valid
        signature (verification below) → creation is DONE; record
        `work release record tag <tag> --sha <merge-sha>` if not yet
        recorded and continue to step 4.
      - pointing ANYWHERE else, or unsigned, or the signature does not
        verify → HARD ERROR. Never re-point, never re-sign, never delete.
        Record a critical error naming the tag, the SHA it points at, and
        the recorded merge SHA; `work commit`; end the session. (The burned
        version runbook in the resume section is the human's repair path.)
- [ ] Create the tag, signed with the provisioned key:

      ```
      git -c gpg.format=ssh -c user.signingkey=/release-signing-key \
          tag -s <tag> <merge-sha> -m "Release <version>"
      ```
- [ ] Verify before recording — the signature against the provisioned key's
      public half, and the target commit against the recorded merge SHA:
      `echo "* $(ssh-keygen -y -f /release-signing-key)" > /tmp/allowed-signers`
      then
      `git -c gpg.format=ssh -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=/tmp/allowed-signers tag -v <tag>`
      and `git rev-parse <tag>^{commit}` must print the merge SHA.
- [ ] Record it: `work release record tag <tag> --sha <merge-sha>`. The
      recorder itself hard-stops unless the SHA equals the recorded merge
      SHA and the name is exactly `v<version>` — a refusal here means the
      tag is wrong: treat it as the hard error above, do not argue with it.

### Step 4 — `leg2-push-github-main` / `leg2-push-github-tag` — GitHub: `main` first, THEN the tag

The ordering is load-bearing: the tag push triggers `release.yml`, and its
verify job asserts the tag equals GitHub `main` HEAD — `main` must land
before the trigger fires. Every push goes through `bin/gate-push`; the
session's push allow list already grants the mirror's branch push and its
tags (the `repo|refs/tags/*` grammar). A gate refusal is a provisioning gap
to report, never something to route around.

- [ ] `main` first — skip when current: `git rev-parse github/main` already
      equal to the merge SHA → record the receipt (if absent) and move on.
      Otherwise, with local `main` checked out at the merge SHA:
      `bin/gate-push --remote github`
      A non-fast-forward rejection here is the divergence hard stop from
      step 2 (the mirror moved between check and push) — same contract, no
      retry, no force.
- [ ] Record: `work release record receipt github-main-push`
- [ ] Then the tag — skip when current:
      `git ls-remote github refs/tags/<tag>` already showing the recorded
      merge SHA → record the receipt (if absent) and move on. Otherwise:
      `bin/gate-push --tag <tag> --remote github`
- [ ] Record: `work release record receipt github-tag-push`

Every `github`-remote command in this step — the `ls-remote` skip check and
both pushes — carries the ASKPASS prefix from the credentials section in
its own shell invocation; nothing persists from the commands before it.
`bin/gate-push` shells out to `git`, so the ASKPASS environment passes
through, but nothing else supplies the credential. A
rejected push (a 403 from a missing or read-only credential, a protection
rule) is a hard error naming the credential requirement — never an
alternative push path, per HARD RULES. Note the ordering cost of getting
this wrong: the tag already exists locally by this point, and the tag is
immutable, so recovery is fixing the credential and re-entering the leg,
never re-tagging.

### Step 5 — `leg2-poll-actions` — the GitHub Actions release run

Find the `release.yml` run for the recorded merge SHA on the mirror
(`gh run list` filtered by workflow and head SHA — the recorded SHA, never
"the latest run").

- [ ] Already green for that SHA → skip the poll; make sure the receipts
      below exist (their URLs must name the run that actually uploaded) and
      move on.
- [ ] Otherwise poll the run to completion (`gh run watch <run-id>`, or a
      loop on `gh run view <run-id>`).
- [ ] Green → PyPI has the version (with attestations) and the GitHub
      Release exists. Record which run actually published:
      `work release record receipt actions-run --url <Actions run URL>`
      `work release record receipt pypi --url <PyPI release URL>`
      `--url` is required on both. On a re-run, record the run that
      UPLOADED, not merely the last green one — `release.yml` publishes
      with `skip-existing`, so a later green run may have uploaded nothing.
- [ ] Red, with refs current → re-dispatch the workflow via the API, keyed
      on the recorded SHA: `gh run rerun <run-id>`. NEVER re-tag to
      retrigger — the tag is immutable: never deleted, never re-pointed,
      never re-signed. Then poll again. A run that cannot be re-run is the
      fail-loudly path below.
- [ ] Red at the **Gate version reuse** step (the version is already on the
      index) → this is NOT an agent decision. Re-running cannot clear it:
      the gate fails closed because a resumed publish and a republish of
      different code are indistinguishable to the pipeline. Report it to
      the human with the Actions run URL via the fail-loudly path below;
      authorizing the resume means an admin setting the
      `RELEASE_RESUME_VERSION` Actions variable to that exact version and
      clearing it afterwards. Never set or ask for a blanket value.
- [ ] Still red → FAIL LOUDLY with the Actions run URL:
      `work state set --stop-reason=critical_error --critical-error
      '{"summary": "GitHub release run red", "detail": "<Actions run URL>"}'`
      then `work commit` and end the session. What to do about the red run
      is the human's call — the single API re-dispatch above is the only
      automatic remediation this leg attempts. Nothing has been published
      internally yet — that is the point of the ordering.

### Step 6 — `leg2-push-gitlab-tag` — the GitLab tag, LAST

Only after the GitHub run is green. Public-before-internal is deliberate: a
GitHub failure leaves no half-released internal state.

- [ ] Skip when current: `git ls-remote origin refs/tags/<tag>` already
      showing the recorded merge SHA → record the receipt (if absent) and
      move on. A GitLab tag at any OTHER SHA is the tag hard error from
      step 3 — never re-point it.
- [ ] Push: `bin/gate-push --tag <tag> --remote origin`
      GitLab's existing tag pipeline publishes the internal package
      registry and container image from there.
- [ ] Record: `work release record receipt gitlab-tag-push`

### Step 7 — receipts complete, run complete

- [ ] `work release status` must show every field recorded and nothing left
      to advance. Fill any receipt gap NOW (`--note` that it was recorded
      after the fact) — an unreceipted push is invisible to the resume
      derivation.
- [ ] Proceed to "Closing out the run" (retro, goal assessment, completion
      state, `work commit`).

### The idempotency ladder (re-entry map)

Leg 2 may be entered any number of times — watch death, container reap,
scheduled relaunch. Every re-entry converges because every step compares its
target to the recorded merge SHA before acting:

| Observed state | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Merge SHA already recorded | Re-record with the freshly re-read version — the mismatch check runs every time |
| Tag exists at the recorded merge SHA, signature valid | Skip creation; ensure the tag record exists |
| Tag exists anywhere else, or unsigned/invalid | HARD ERROR — never re-point, never re-sign, never delete |
| GitHub `main` already at the merge SHA | Skip the push; ensure the receipt exists |
| GitHub tag ref already at the merge SHA | Skip the push; ensure the receipt exists |
| Actions run green for the recorded SHA | Skip the poll; receipts must name the run that uploaded |
| Actions run red, refs current | Re-dispatch via the API, keyed on the recorded SHA — never re-tag |
| GitLab tag already at the merge SHA | Skip the push; ensure the receipt exists |
| Every receipt recorded | Nothing to advance — close out the run |
